    "explanation" : "Object deserialization of untrusted data can lead to remote code execution, if there is a class in classpath allow the trigger of malicious operation.\n\nLibraries developers tend to fix class that provided potential malicious trigger. Their are still classes that are known to trigger [Denial of Service](https://gist.github.com/coekie/a27cc406fc9f3dc7a70d).\n\nDeserialization is a sensible operation that has a great history of vulnerabilities. The web application might become vulnerable has soon as a new vulnerability is found in the Java Virtual Machine.\n\nCode at risk:\n\n```\npublic UserData deserializeObject(InputStream receivedFile) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {\n\n    try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(receivedFile)) {\n        return (UserData) in.readObject();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSolution:\n\n```\nAvoid deserializing object provided by remote users.\n```\n\nMore information at:\n\n* [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n* [Deserialization of untrusted data](https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n* [Serialization and Deserialization](http://www.oracle.com/technetwork/java/seccodeguide-139067.html#8)\n* [A proof-of-concept tool for generating payloads that exploit unsafe Java object deserialization](https://github.com/frohoff/ysoserial)\n* [OpenJDK: Deserialization issue in ObjectInputStream.readSerialData() (CVE-2015-2590)](https://bugzilla.redhat.com/show_bug.cgi?id=CVE-2015-2590)\n* [Rapid7: Sun Java Calendar Deserialization Privilege Escalation (CVE-2008-5353)](https://www.rapid7.com/db/modules/exploit/multi/browser/java_calendar_deserialize)",
